cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "GPIE — Green Policy Intelligence Engine: What Does a Country-Level Difference-in-Differences on Satellite NO2 Identify?"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GPIE"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GPIE"
abstract: >-
  GPIE tests what a country-level difference-in-differences (DiD) on satellite
  NO2 identifies when treated and comparison countries differ in baseline
  pollution. It uses Sentinel-5P TROPOMI tropospheric NO2 (2,192 daily 0.1 degree
  rasters, 2019-2024) and, as a negative-control case, the European Climate Law
  (Regulation (EU) 2021/1119, adopted 30 June 2021), comparing the EU-27 with nine
  neighbouring non-EU countries. The country-level DiD finds NO2 fell 1.34e-6
  mol/m2 more in the EU-27 (4.3% of its pre-treatment level, p = 0.022), but the
  estimate is not significant without the winter months, with area weights, with
  log GDP, with real GDP per capita or under randomization inference on the
  coefficient, and it appears only
  from the third year after the law. Under random assignment of the treatment
  label the estimate follows the pre-treatment gap between groups (r = -0.81), and
  comparing EU and comparison pixels that started at the same NO2 level reduces it
  to -0.21e-6 (95% CI -0.67 to +0.24); a known 2.5% reduction added to the data
  is detected by the same comparison. Against 1,543 EEA background stations the
  satellite values agree (monthly r = 0.68), and the stations show that a
  December 2023 drop in the satellite data is a retrieval artefact. Polluted areas lost about 15-25% of their
  NO2 in north-western Europe, including the non-EU United Kingdom and
  Switzerland, and little in south-eastern Europe, including EU members, and in
  western Turkey; the cause of this pattern is not identified. Country-level DiD
  on satellite NO2 should be checked against comparisons matched on baseline
  pollution before it is read as a policy effect.
keywords:
  - nitrogen dioxide
  - TROPOMI
  - Sentinel-5P
  - difference-in-differences
  - parallel trends
  - aggregation
  - modifiable areal unit problem
  - satellite remote sensing
  - policy evaluation
  - geospatial analysis
license: CC-BY-4.0
version: 1.2.0
date-released: "2026-08-14"
